**Read:** [Second Level Course in Heat Transfer — R. P. Vedula](../sources/Second-Level-Heat-Transfer-Vedula.lingua#r_663BY2CF56BBB35C1S595A2VRF), the complete two-phase equations and boiling sequence; [A Heat Transfer Textbook — Lienhard and Lienhard](../sources/A-Heat-Transfer-Textbook-Lienhard.lingua#r_7TKQ2D5YRNQ8XVHXNJQDRANWKD), flow boiling and phase-change limits; [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1), equilibrium quality and phase properties.

Mass quality, void fraction and phase velocities are different quantities. A homogeneous equilibrium flow approximation imposes relations among them; separated or nonequilibrium models require additional information and closure. Define the averaging and phase-change terms before writing a two-phase energy account.

Pressure loss, heat input, compressibility and changing phase fraction can couple strongly. Flow regimes and instabilities affect wall wetting, transfer rates and measurement interpretation. A steady local correlation can miss an oscillating or dryout-prone system.

Compare a homogeneous model with a more detailed documented method for one bounded case. Check mass, energy and pressure behavior, and identify the required slip, interfacial-area or transfer closure. Boiler, evaporator and multiphase process design continue in the full engineering routes.

